---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../../data/site";
import { blogPostingSchema } from "../../lib/schema";

const slug = "flood-advisory-vs-watch-vs-warning";
const title = "Flood Advisory vs Flood Watch vs Flood Warning Explained";
const description =
  "NWS flood terms are a three-step scale: advisory, watch, and warning. Understand what each means for Louisiana and Mississippi residents and when to move to higher ground.";
const canonical = `${site.url}/blog/${slug}/`;
const date = "2026-08-07";
const jsonLd = blogPostingSchema({
  title,
  description,
  url: canonical,
  datePublished: date,
  dateModified: date,
  authorName: "HurricaneHub team"
});
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} jsonLd={jsonLd} lastModified={date} ogImage="https://www.hurricanetracker.cc/images/blog/og/flood-advisory-vs-watch-vs-warning.png">
  <main class="prose">
    <article>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Flooding causes more hurricane deaths than wind. Yet the three NWS flood terms —
        <strong>advisory</strong>, <strong>watch</strong>, and <strong>warning</strong> — are routinely mixed
        up. Each step up the scale means water is closer to you and you have less time to react.
      </p>

      <h2>Flood Advisory: Nuisance, But Pay Attention</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>flood advisory</strong> is the lowest tier. It means minor flooding is occurring or expected
        — the kind that pools on roads, fills ditches, and turns low underpasses into hazards. No immediate
        threat to life is expected, but driving through standing water is never safe. "Turn around, don't
        drown" applies even at the advisory level.
      </p>
      <p>
        Along the low-lying parishes tracked on the
        <a href="/tracker/louisiana/">Louisiana flood risk</a> page, advisories are common during
        routine summer storms. Treat them as a reminder to avoid flood-prone commutes and to keep drains clear.
      </p>

      <h2>Flood Watch: Be Ready to Act</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>flood watch</strong> means conditions are favorable for flooding within the next several
        hours to a day. Flooding is not happening yet, but the atmosphere is primed for it — heavy rain rates,
        saturated ground, or a surge scenario. During a watch, move valuables off the floor, charge devices,
        and identify higher ground.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because the <a href="/tracker/mississippi/">Mississippi hurricane tracker</a> coastline sits
        at the confluence of river and Gulf systems, a flood watch there can escalate quickly when rainfall
        bands stall. A watch is the time to decide: if water starts rising, where will my household go?
      </p>

      <h2>Flood Warning: Water Is Here or Imminent</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>flood warning</strong> means flooding is occurring, is imminent, or has a very high
        probability. This is the act-now tier. If you are in a warned area — especially a flash-flood or storm-
        surge warning zone — move to higher ground immediately and do not wait for the water to arrive.
      </p>
      <p>
        Storm-surge flood warnings deserve special respect: surge can outrun a car and arrives ahead of peak
        winds. When a warning names your zone, evacuation orders are often already in effect.
      </p>

      <h2>How to Read the Three Tiers at a Glance</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Advisory</strong> — minor flooding likely; avoid water on roads, stay aware.</li>
        <li><strong>Watch</strong> — flooding possible; prepare, move items up, pick higher ground.</li>
        <li><strong>Warning</strong> — flooding happening or imminent; move to safety now.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The simplest memory aid: advisory is "be careful," watch is "get ready," warning is "go." When in doubt
        during a hurricane, always defer to the more serious term and to local official instructions.
      </p>

      <p class="byline">
        This article was updated for the 2026 hurricane season by the HurricaneHub team.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2rem 1.25rem 3rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .prose h1 {
    font-size: 2rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .prose h2 {
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    margin: 2rem 0 0.75rem;
  }
  .lead {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--muted, #4a5568);
  }
  .prose a {
    color: var(--accent, #1f6feb);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  .byline {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding-top: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border, #e2e8f0);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--muted, #4a5568);
  }
</style>
